>
  <h2>What changed in ISO 9001:2026?</h2>
  <p>
    ISO published the sixth edition of ISO 9001 on 16 September 2026, after a Final Draft International
    Standard approved with strong international support in August 2026. Every review of it, from certification
    bodies to independent consultants, describes it the same way: an evolutionary, measured revision rather
    than a rebuild. The Harmonized Structure is untouched. Clause 4 is still context of the organisation,
    clause 8 is still operation, and the certification logic behind an ISO 9001 audit has not changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    What has changed is language, emphasis and a handful of clauses. The two verbs "maintain" and "retain"
    that governed documented information are unified into a single phrase. Quality culture and ethical
    behaviour become explicit leadership and awareness requirements. The 2024 climate-change amendment is
    folded into the core text instead of standing apart from it. Clause 6.1 separates risk from opportunity.
    Clause 10 is restructured from three subclauses to two. None of this asks a small company to tear up its
    quality management system and start again. See the checklist below for what needs doing.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the ISO 9001:2026 transition period?</h2>
  <p>
    The transition period is three years from publication. ISO 9001:2015 certificates stay valid, and can
    continue to be audited and issued against, for about three years from the 16 September 2026 publication
    date, so by the end of September 2029, organisations are expected to have moved across to the 2026
    edition. That figure is consistent across the standard’s own committee page and independent certification
    bodies, so treat it as the planning date to work back from.
  </p>
  <p>
    Certification bodies themselves have to complete their own accreditation to audit against the 2026 edition
    before they can offer it to clients, which takes them time after publication. In practice that means most
    organisations will not be asked to move immediately: your existing surveillance and recertification cycle
    against ISO 9001:2015 continues to run, and your certification body will tell you when the 2026 edition
    becomes an option, and eventually the only option, for your audits.
  </p>

  <h2>Do I need to recertify to ISO 9001:2026?</h2>
  <p>
    Not immediately, and not on your own timetable. Your current ISO 9001:2015 certificate remains valid
    through the transition window described above, so there is no need to book a special recertification
    audit the moment the 2026 edition is published. The practical first step is to ask your
    certification body when they expect to be ready to audit against the new edition, and where that falls
    relative to your next scheduled audit.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is worth doing now, well ahead of any deadline, is the gap assessment in the checklist further down
    this guide. Most of what changed is wording rather than new work, so a small company’s transition is
    usually a documentation and awareness exercise.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed for document control under clause 7.5?</h2>
  <p>
    Document control keeps its clause number and its three-part structure: 7.5.1 general, 7.5.2 creating and
    updating, 7.5.3 control of documented information. Nothing about that numbering or structure moves in
    ISO 9001:2026, and the substance of what you have to do is reported as largely unchanged from the 2015
    edition. See our guide to <a href="/guides/iso-9001-document-control">ISO 9001 document control</a> for
    the full clause 7.5 breakdown, which still applies.
  </p>
  <p>
    The change that does run through 7.5, and through the rest of the standard with it, is terminology. ISO
    9001:2015 asked you to <strong>maintain</strong> documents (keep them current as your process
    changes) and <strong>retain</strong> records (keep evidence of what happened, unchanged).
    ISO 9001:2026 replaces both verbs with one phrase: information must be <strong>"available as documented
    information"</strong>, or "available as evidence of" a given activity. It is described as a clarity change.
  </p>
  <p>
    In practice, that means two things for a small company. First, your document and record controls, such as
    version history, approvals, retention and protecting documents from unauthorised change, do
    not need to change, because the underlying requirement has not moved. Second, if your own document control
    procedure quotes the standard’s old "maintain" and "retain" wording directly, update that wording so it
    matches the current edition. Our <a href="/guides/document-control-procedure">document control procedure
    example</a> shows what a procedure covering this looks like end to end.
  </p>

  <h2>What other changes matter for a small company?</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond document control, a handful of other changes are corroborated across multiple independent reviews
    of the standard. Each is a real change in emphasis, and each has a modest, practical response for a
    20 to 200 person company rather than a large project.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
